course: dictionary
lesson: 4666
title: Hogweed and the danger of touching
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you can picture, and some words carry a warning. «Giant hogweed is a very tall plant with huge white flower umbrellas, often beside rivers.» Tall, white flowers and rivers give you pictures, so you know where to look.
explain: Read the whole sentence to find the clue. «Giant hogweed can grow taller than a grown-up, and its sap makes skin burn and blister in sunlight, so it must never be touched.» The word so shows the reason: the sap is harmful, so we must never touch the plant.
explain: The word giant is a clue too. Hogweed is the name of the plant, and giant tells you it is a huge, dangerous kind. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you are right.
fact: Hogweed is a tall wild plant with big flat heads of tiny white flowers.
fact: Giant hogweed is a huge kind whose sap makes skin burn and blister badly in sunlight.
fact: If sap gets on your skin, cover the skin from the sun, wash it with soap and water, and tell an adult.
fact: Giant hogweed grows by rivers, roadsides and parks in the UK, Ireland and North America.
fact: «Sosnowsky's hogweed», like giant hogweed, has sap that makes skin burn in sunlight, and it has spread across Ukraine from old farm fields.
exercise: «Giant hogweed is a very tall plant with huge white flower umbrellas, often beside rivers.» Where is giant hogweed often found? | In the sea | *Beside rivers | On the moon | Underground in caves
exercise: Hogweed is a tall wild plant with big flat heads of what? | *Tiny white flowers | Thick brown bark | Yellow petals | Large red berries
exercise: «Giant hogweed can grow taller than a grown-up, and its sap makes skin burn and blister in sunlight, so it must never be touched.» What does the word so tell you? | *It shows the reason: the sap is harmful | It means very quickly | It starts a question | It means a long time ago
test: If hogweed sap gets on your skin, what should you do first? | Stay in the sunlight | *Cover the skin from the sun | Eat a leaf to feel better | Rub it with sand
test: What does blister mean in «its sap makes skin burn and blister in sunlight»? | A cold drink | A bit of soap | A loud noise | *A raised bubble on the skin
exam: Jayden says giant hogweed is just a daisy and safe to touch. What is the best answer? | Yes, it is a daisy and safe to touch | No, it is dangerous: its sap burns skin in sunlight | Yes, you should eat it | *No, it is dangerous: its sap burns skin in sunlight, so never touch it
